public class Enemy
{
    public string Name { get; }                  // read-only property, set in the constructor
    public int Health { get; private set; }      // anyone can read, only Enemy can change

    public Enemy(string name, int health)
    {
        Name = name;
        Health = health;
    }

    public bool IsDead => Health <= 0;           // a computed property

    public void TakeDamage(int amount)
    {
        if (amount < 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(amount));
        Health = Math.Max(0, Health - amount);
    }

    public override string ToString() => $"{Name} ({Health} HP)";
}

var goblin = new Enemy("Goblin", 30);
goblin.TakeDamage(12);
Console.WriteLine(goblin);      // Goblin (18 HP)
```

- **Properties** (`{ get; private set; }`) are C#'s answer to Java's getters and setters: other code reads `goblin.Health` but can't write it, so health can never go negative by accident.
- `=>` makes one-line members.
- `ToString()` controls how it prints.

This is exactly how Unity scripts are shaped: a class per kind of thing (enemy, turret, player), fields for its state, methods for what it does.

